            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_c\\) are pairwise distinct, each has slope \\(-1\\) (hence is not\nsunny), and  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1}\\bigl(L_c\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\), and contains\nexactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line  \n\nFix a diagonal \\(L_c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  \nThe point  \n\n\\[\nP_c=(c-1,1)\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(L_c\\) because \\((c-1)+1=c\\).\n\nConsider the line through the origin \\((0,0)\\) and \\(P_c\\):\n\n\\[\nS_c:\\;y=\\frac{1}{c-1}\\,x .\n\\]\n\n*The slope* of \\(S_c\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{c-1}\\).  \nSince \\(c\\ge2\\), this number is finite, non‑zero and different from \\(-1\\); thus\n\\(S_c\\) is sunny.\n\nNow replace \\(L_c\\) by \\(S_c\\) in the family \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).  Denote the new\nfamily by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_c=(\\mathcal F_0\\setminus\\{L_c\\})\\cup\\{S_c\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal F_c\\) still covers all points of \\(T_n\\):\n\n* The point \\(P_c\\) (the only point of \\(L_c\\) that could be lost) belongs to\n  \\(S_c\\).\n* Every other point of \\(L_c\\) has the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and\n  \\((a,b)\\neq P_c\\).  Such a point also lies on the diagonal \\(L_{a+b}=L_c\\),\n  but because \\(a+b=c\\) it lies on a *different* diagonal \\(L_{c'}\\) with\n  \\(c'\\neq c\\) (namely \\(c'=a+b\\) where \\(a\\neq c-1\\) or \\(b\\neq1\\)).  That\n  diagonal remains in \\(\\mathcal F_c\\), so the point is still covered.\n\nThe line \\(S_c\\) is distinct from every remaining diagonal because each\ndiagonal has slope \\(-1\\) whereas \\(S_c\\) has a positive slope.  Hence\n\\(\\mathcal F_c\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is\nsunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines), choose any subset\n\\(\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) of size \\(k\\) (where\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).  Replace each diagonal \\(L_{c_i}\\) by the sunny line\n\\(S_{c_i}\\) as described in Section 2.  After all replacements we obtain the\nfamily  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\\bigl(\\mathcal F_0\\setminus\\{L_{c_1},\\dots ,L_{c_k}\\}\\bigr)\n            \\cup\\{S_{c_1},\\dots ,S_{c_k}\\}.\n\\]\n\n* The family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the\n  \\(S_{c_i}\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonals.\n* By the argument of Section 2, every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered.\n* All lines are distinct: the diagonals have slope \\(-1\\), while each\n  \\(S_{c_i}\\) has a distinct positive slope \\(\\frac{1}{c_i-1}\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of other values  \n\nSince the total number of lines is fixed at \\(n\\), the number of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n\\) nor be negative.  Hence no value of \\(k\\) outside the\ninterval \\([0,n]\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
