of the longest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(3\\le i\\le k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\text{ the line through }(2,1)\\text{ and }(i,n+1-i).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(i-n\\); because \\(i\\neq n-1\\) (the case \\(i=n-1\\) would give slope \\(-1\\)), each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nWe keep the rows \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (which are non‑sunny) and add the sunny lines \\(L_3,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \nEvery point of the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) is covered by exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\); all other points belong to one of the retained rows.  Hence we obtain a covering with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-1=(i-n)(x-2).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  \nIts slope \\(i-n\\) is never \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\); it equals \\(-1\\) only when \\(i=n-1\\).  In that exceptional case we replace \\(L_{n-1}\\) by the vertical line \\(x=2\\) (which is non‑sunny).  Consequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2},L_{n},\\;x=2\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are sunny except possibly the vertical one; by discarding the vertical line and keeping the remaining \\(n\\) sunny lines we obtain a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\) (which joins \\((2,1)\\) with \\((a,n+1-a)\\)).  Hence the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_a\\mid a=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) cover the whole set \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6  The special case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe constructions of §§2.1–2.5 give \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \nA covering with three sunny lines is obtained by\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y-2=-\\tfrac1