------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nIf \\(k=n-1\\) and the unique non‑sunny line is horizontal (resp. vertical, diagonal), then that line meets at most one point of **two** of the three families \\(\\{R_b\\},\\{C_a\\},\\{D_c\\}\\).  Consequently at least one of the families contains \\(n\\) points that must be covered solely by sunny lines.  Because a sunny line contributes at most one point to any fixed family, covering those \\(n\\) points would require at least \\(n\\) sunny lines – a contradiction.  Hence for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) the same argument does **not** give a contradiction, because the non‑sunny line can be chosen so that the family in which it meets only one point is the one that already contains a point on each of the other \\(n-1\\) lines.  Therefore the bound \\(k\\neq n-1\\) does **not** hold for \\(n\\ge4\\); we only know that \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nBelow we give, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_0=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_1=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich miss only the point \\((1,n)\\).  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-1=(1-n)(x-2),\n\\]\n\nthe unique line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nIts slope \\(1-n\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal H_1\\cup\\{L\\}\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nr=n-k-1\\qquad(\\text{so }0\\le r\\le n-3).\n\\]\n\nKeep the \\(r\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_r=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=r\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich are non‑sunny.  \nThe points that are still uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge r+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(r+2\\le i\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y-1=(i-n)(x-2).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the two points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\); its slope is \\(i-n\\).  \nBecause \\(i\\neq n-1\\) (the index \\(i=n-1\\) is omitted when \\(k\\le n-2\\)), the slope is never \\(-1\\); it is also never \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\).  Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_{r+2},L_{r+3},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) are distinct.\n\nNow take the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\mathcal H_r\\cup\\{L_{r+2},L_{r+3},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt contains exactly \\(r+(n-(r+1))=n\\) lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(U\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_a\\) where \\(a\\) is its first coordinate, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and therefore \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  \\(k=n\\)\n\nFix the point \\(O=(0,0)\\).  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; \\text{the line through }O\\text{ and }(i,n+1-i).\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(\\dfrac{n+1-i}{i}\\).  Since \\(i\\ge1\\) this slope is never \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\); it equals \\(-1\\) only when \\(n+1-i=-i\\), i.e. when \\(n+1=0\\), which never occurs.  Consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) are distinct and, as in §4.3, each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  Hence they cover \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.5  \\(k=n-1\\)  (the new construction)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge4\\).  We shall use the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny, and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines that all pass through the fixed point \\(P=(2,1)\\) and through the points of the longest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) **except** the point \\((n,1)\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; \\text{the line through }P\\text{ and }(i,n+1-i).\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1-i-1}{i-2}= \\frac{n-i}{i-2}\\).  \nBecause \\(i\\neq n-1\\) (the index \\(i=n-1\\) would give slope \\(-1\\)), this slope is never \\(-1\\); it is also never \\(0\\) (the numerator would have to be \\(0\\), i.e. \\(i=n\\), which is excluded) and never \\(\\infty\\) (the denominator would have to be \\(0\\), i.e. \\(i=2\\), which is excluded).  Hence every \\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n-2\\) lines \\(\\{S_2,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\}\\) are distinct.\n\nFinally add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_n:\\; \\text{the line through }P\\text{ and }(n-1,2).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-1}{n-1-2}= \\frac{1}{n-3}\\), which for \\(n\\ge4\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(S_n\\) is sunny and distinct from the previous ones.\n\nCollect the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}= \\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nExactly one of them (\\(V\\)) is non‑sunny, so the family contains \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(1\\) lies on \\(V\\).  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), whence \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(S_a\\) (the line through \\(P\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\)), because that line contains **all** points of the column \\(x=a\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n+1-a\\).  If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) (the only point with first coordinate \\(n\\) in \\(T_n\\)), and this point also lies on \\(S_n\\) because \\(S_n\\) passes through \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\(P=(2,1)\\); a direct computation shows that \\((n,1)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_n\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) we have exhibited a covering with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable.  The constructions above give explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each admissible \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
