overs all of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for any \\(n\\ge5\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The cases \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\)\n\n#### 3.1  \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,2),(2,2),(1,3).\n\\]\n\n*Existence.*  \n- \\(k=0\\): three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\).  \n- \\(k=1\\): two horizontals \\(y=1,2\\) and one sunny line, e.g. \\(y=x+1\\).  \n- \\(k=3\\): Lemma 1 with \\(m=3\\) gives three sunny lines covering \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines.  \nEach sunny line meets a given horizontal row in at most one point; therefore the three\npoints of the bottom row \\(\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\}\\) can be covered by at most two sunny\nlines, leaving at least one of them uncovered.  That uncovered point would have to lie\non the third (non‑sunny) line, which must be horizontal.  Consequently the two sunny\nlines would have to cover the remaining five points, but any sunny line can contain at\nmost two points of \\(T_3\\) (different rows and columns).  Two sunny lines can thus\ncover at most four points, a contradiction.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\nTherefore for \\(n=3\\) the admissible set is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n#### 3.2  \\(n=4\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_4\\) contains ten points.  \n\n*Existence.*  \n- \\(k=0\\): four horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3,4\\).  \n- \\(k=1\\): three horizontals and one sunny line (e.g. \\(y=x+1\\)).  \n- \\(k=4\\): Lemma 1 with \\(m=4\\).  \n- \\(k=3\\): the explicit construction from the counter‑example:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &y=1 \\quad(\\text{non‑sunny}),\\\\\n  &y=x+1,\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\; y=-2x+6 \\quad(\\text{sunny}).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  These four distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_4\\).\n\n*Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \nIf exactly two sunny lines are used, then \\