e $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_m|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n2. **Reduction.**  \n   Suppose we want a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.  \n   Let \\(h=n-k\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.  \n   Choose the \\(h\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(h\\).  \n   After translating the uncovered points down by \\(h\\) units we obtain precisely the set\n   \\(T_k\\).  \n   Consequently a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) exists **iff** the triangular set \\(T_k\\) can be\n   covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n3. **Covering \\(T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines ( \\(k\\neq 2\\) ).**  \n   Let \\(k\\ge 3\\). Choose a prime \\(p>k\\).  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{p-i}\\,x+\\frac{p}{p-i}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *All \\(L_i\\) are sunny*: the slope \\(\\frac{i}{p-i}\\) is non‑zero, finite and different fro