hese lines belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\) (they are the only non‑sunny lines, because a\nvertical line would be parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore not sunny).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(h\\) are covered by (1).\nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nT'_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge h+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a,b-h)\\) is a bijection from \\(T'_k\\) onto \\(T_k\\).  Hence,\nafter translating the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\}\\) downward by\n\\(h\\) units, we obtain a family of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that covers\n\\(T_k\\).\n\nConversely, if we can cover \\(T_k\\) with \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, then by adding the\n\\(h=n-k\\) horizontal lines (1) and translating the sunny lines upward by \\(h\\) units we\nobtain a configuration for \\((n,k)\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A configuration for }(n,k)\\text{ exists }\\Longleftrightarrow\nT_k\\text{ can be covered by }k\\text{ sunny lines}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is reduced to determining for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) the\ntriangular set \\(T_k\\) admits a covering by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Covering \\(T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\n#### 2.1  The cases \\(k=0,1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the empty family covers the empty set \\(T_0\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): the single line through \\((1,1)\\) with any admissible slope (for instance\n  \\(y=2x-1\\)) covers the unique point of \\(T_1\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k=2\\) – impossibility\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(1,2)\\}\\).  \nNo two sunny lines can contain these three points, because any line containing two of\nthem would be either horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\).  Hence \\(T_2\\) cannot be\ncovered by two sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.3  The case \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\ge3\\) and let \\(p\\) be a prime with \\(p>k\\) (such a prime exists by Euclid’s\ntheorem).  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{p-i}\\,x+\\frac{p}{p-i}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*All \\(L_i\\) are sunny.*  \nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{p-i}\\) is non‑zero, finite and different from \\(-1\\) because\n\\(0<i<p\\).  Consequently each \\(L_i\\) is a sunny line.\n\n*The lines are distinct.*  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then \\(\\dfrac{i}{p-i}\\neq\\dfrac{j}{p-j}\\); therefore the lines have\ndifferent slopes and are distinct.\n\n**Lemma 2.** Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1<p\\), the integer  \n\n\\[\ni\\;:=\\;a(p-b)\\pmod p\n\\qquad (1\\le i\\le p-1)\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(i\\le k\\).  Indeed,\n\\(0<a(p-b)<p(p-1)\\) and reduction modulo \\(p\\) yields a representative \\(i\\) in the\ninterval \\([1,p-1]\\); the inequality \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) forces \\(i\\le k\\).\n\nNow substitute this \\(i\\) into (3):\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{p-i}\\,a+\\frac{p}{p-i}\n\\Longleftrightarrow\ni(p-b)=a(p-i),\n\\]\nwhich is precisely the defining congruence for \\(i\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the\nequation of \\(L_i\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  Uniqueness follows from the\ndistinctness of the slopes.  ∎\n\nLemma 2 shows that the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).  Since\neach \\(L_i\\) is sunny, we have exhibited a covering of \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines for every \\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (2) and the results of Section 2 we obtain:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always possible (use only horizontal lines, or one sunny line\n  together with horizontals).\n* \\(k=2\\) is never possible (Section 2.2).\n* Every integer \\(k\\ge3\\) is possible (Lemma 2).\n\nTherefore, for a given \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3