[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_m|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\)\n(vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point\nof \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Let  \n\n\\[\nh:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\]\n\nEvery non‑sunny line has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  Replace each of them by a\nhorizontal line \\(y=r\\) where \\(r\\) is a positive integer not used before.\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) has a positive integer \\(y\\)-coordinate at most \\(n\\),\nthe replacement does not lose any covered point: a point \\((a,b)\\) that was on a\nvertical or slope \\(-1\\) line is also on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), and a point\nalready on a horizontal line remains covered.  After the replacement we have\n\\(h\\) distinct horizontal lines; we may assume they are\n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies\n\\(b\\le h\\).  The uncovered points are\n\n\\[\nT'_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge h+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a,b-h)\\) is a bijection from \\(T'_k\\) onto \\(T_k\\).  Hence,\nif we translate the remaining \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) downward by \\(h\\) units,\nwe obtain a family of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that covers \\(T_k\\).\n\nConversely, if \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, then adding the\n\\(h=n-k\\) horizontal lines (1) and translating the sunny lines upward by \\(h\\) units\nproduces a configuration for \\((n,k)\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A configuration for }(n,k)\\text{ exists }\\Longleftrightarrow\nT_k\\text{ can be covered by }k\\text{ sunny lines}.}\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem reduces to determining for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) the\nset \\(T_k\\) admits a covering by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Covering \\(T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\n##### 2.1  The cases \\(k=0,1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the empty family covers the empty set \\(T_0\\).\n* \\(k=1\\): the line through \\((1,1)\\) with any admissible slope (e.g. \\(y=2x-1\\))\n  covers the unique point of \\(T_1\\).\n\n##### 2.2  The case \\(k=2\\) – impossibility\n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(1,2)\\}\\).  \nAny line through two of these points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\)\n(vertical) or \\(-1\\); all three are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence no two\nsunny lines can contain the three points, and \\(T_2\\) cannot be covered by two sunny\nlines.  Consequently, by (2), \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n##### 2.3  The case \\(k\\ge3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\ge3\\) and let \\(p\\) be a prime with \\(p>k\\) (such a prime exists by Euclid’s\ntheorem).  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{p-i}\\,x+\\frac{p}{p-i}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*All \\(L_i\\) are sunny.*  \nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{p-i}\\) is non‑zero, finite and different from \\(-1\\) because\n\\(0<i<p\\).\n\n*The lines are distinct.*  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then \\(\\dfrac{i}{p-i}\\neq\\dfrac{j}{p-j}\\); therefore the lines have\ndifferent slopes.\n\n**Lemma.** Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1<p\\), the integer  \n\n\\[\ni\\equiv a(p-b)\\pmod p,\\qquad 1\\le i\\le p-1,\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(i\\le k\\).  Substituting this \\(i\\) into (3) yields\n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{i}{p-i}a+\\frac{p}{p-i}\n\\Longleftrightarrow\ni(p-b)=a(p-i),\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the defining congruence for \\(i\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the\nequation of \\(L_i\\).  Uniqueness follows from the distinctness of the slopes.\n∎\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) cover the whole set \\(T_k\\) for every\n\\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Determination of the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (2) and the results of §2 we obtain:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always possible.\n* \\(k=2\\) is never possible.\n* Every integer \\(k\\ge3\\) with \\(k\\le n\\) is possible (Lemma of §2.3).\n\nHence for a given \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting this set explicitly for the small values of \\(n\\) gives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Verification of the construction\n\nGiven \\(n\\) and a permissible \\(k\\):\n\n1. Take the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).\n2. If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) use the trivial sunny line(s); if \\(k\\ge3\\) use the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) from §2.3.\n3. Translate the sunny lines upward by \\(n-k\\) units; their slopes are unchanged,\n   so they remain sunny.\n\nThe resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\nsunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\) by construction.  This completes the\nproof."
