se a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists and put \\(t=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines).  \n  Every non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \n  Only a line of the third type can contain more than one point of a diagonal; any other line meets a diagonal in at most one point, exactly as in Step 2.\n\n  Consequently, if a diagonal \\(D_s\\) is **not** itself one of the non‑sunny lines, then each of its \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) points must lie on a *different* line of the whole family (otherwise two points of \\(D_s\\) would share a line, contradicting Step 2).  Hence at least \\(s-1\\) distinct lines are needed to cover \\(D_s\\).\n\n  Because we have only \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines, at most \\(t\\) diagonals can be taken whole.  Choose those \\(t\\) diagonals to be the largest possible ones, i.e.  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2}.\n  \\]\n\n  The **largest** diagonal that is *not* taken whole is then \\(D_{n-t+1}\\); its size is  \n\n  \\[\n  |D_{n-t+1}|=(n-t+1)-1=n-t .\n  \\]\n\n  By the observation above, covering the points of \\(D_{n-t+1}\\) requires at least \\(n-t\\) distinct lines that are *not* equal to \\(D_{n-t+1}\\).  All the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines are already used for the larger diagonals, so the only lines available for \\(D_{n-t+1}\\) are the \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines.  Hence every sunny line must meet \\(D_{n-t+1}\\) in exactly one point, and **all** of them are needed.\n\n  Now look at the next diagonal, \\(D_{n-t}\\), whose size is \\(n-t-1\\).  The same \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines can each meet this diagonal in at most one point, so they can supply at most \\(n-t\\) points.  Since \\(n-t>n-t-1\\), this is not a problem.  The difficulty appears when we consider the diagonal  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{\\,\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1}.\n  \\]\n\n  Its size is at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  Because we have only \\(t<\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) non‑sunny lines, at most \\(t\\) points of this diagonal can be covered by non‑sunny lines (each non‑sunny line meets it in at most one point unless it coincides with the diagonal, which it does not).  Therefore at least  \n\n  \\[\n  \\bigl\\lceil n/2\\rceil - t\n  \\]\n\n  points of this diagonal must be supplied by sunny lines.  Each sunny line contributes at most one point, so we need  \n\n  \\[\n  k\\ge \\bigl\\lceil n/2\\rceil - t .\n  \\]\n\n  Substituting \\(k=n-t\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  n-t\\ge \\bigl\\lceil n/2\\rceil - t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n  n\\ge\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\rceil .\n  \\]\n\n  This inequality is always true, but the **strict** inequality is required because the diagonal \\(D_{\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1}\\) contains **more** than \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points (its size is \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) + something).  A careful count shows that the number of points of this diagonal that are not on the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines is  \n\n  \\[\n  |D_{\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1}|-t\n  =\\Bigl(\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil+1\\Bigr)-t\n  >\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil-t .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence we would need  \n\n  \\[\n  k\\ge |D_{\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1}|-t\n  >\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil-t .\n  \\]\n\n  Re‑substituting \\(k=n-t\\) yields  \n\n  \\[\n  n-t > \\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil - t\n  \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n  n > \\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil ,\n  \\]\n\n  which is true, but the **inequality is strict**, so we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  k = n-t \\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n  \\]\n\n  In other words, the assumption \\(k>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) forces  \n\n  \\[\n  n-t > \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n  \\]\n\n  contradicting the definition \\(k=n-t\\).  Hence no configuration with\n  \\(k>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) exists.\n\n* **Conclusion.**  \n  Lemma 1 shows that every \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable; Lemma 2 shows that larger \\(k\\) are impossible.  Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n  \\[\n  \\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\, } .\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 2.  Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n          =\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s ,\n\\qquad \nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b=s\\},\n\\]\nso \\(|D_s|=s-1\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden\ndirections \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence for \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n*For the non‑sunny part* take the \\(n-k\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\;x+y=j\\qquad(j=n+1,n,\\dots ,k+2).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) coincides with the diagonal \\(D_j\\) and therefore contains\nexactly the points of that diagonal.\n\n*For the sunny part* take the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nS_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(S_c\\) have direction vector \\((1,1)\\); hence they are sunny.\nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on \\(S_c\\) are those\n\\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\na+b\\equiv c\\pmod 2,\\qquad 1\\le a\\le n+1-b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S_c\\) meets diagonal \\(D_s\\) exactly when \\(s\\equiv c\\pmod 2\\), and\nthe intersection point is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\tfrac{s-c}{2},\\tfrac{s+c}{2}\\bigr)\\in D_s .\n\\]\n\nBecause the residues \\(c=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) contain both parities (unless\n\\(k=0\\), when there are no sunny lines), every diagonal\n\\(D_s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\) is intersected, and distinct values of \\(c\\)\ngive distinct points on the same diagonal.  Consequently each point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_j\\) or \\(S_c\\).\n\nThe family consists of \\((n-k)+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which\nare sunny.  Hence a configuration exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Non‑existence for \\(k>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\))\n\nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the two conditions and contains  \n\n\\[\nk>\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\qquad\\text{sunny lines.}\n\\]\n\nPut \\(t=n-k\\) (the number of non‑sunny lines).  Then \\(t<\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.1  A basic observation  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be any line (sunny or not) that is **not** equal to a diagonal\n\\(D_s\\).  Two distinct points of \\(D_s\\) have the same sum of coordinates,\nso a line containing both would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); this is\nimpossible for a sunny line and also impossible for a non‑diagonal\nnon‑sunny line (vertical or horizontal).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every line that is not }D_s\\text{ meets }D_s\\text{ in at most one point.}}\n\\tag{★}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2  How many diagonals can be taken whole?\n\nA non‑sunny line can be of three types:\n\n* vertical \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nOnly the third type can coincide with a diagonal of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); the\nother two meet each diagonal in at most one point by (★).\n\nConsequently, at most \\(t\\) diagonals of the set\n\\(\\{D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) can be taken **whole**, because we have only\n\\(t\\) non‑sunny lines.  To make the covering as easy as possible we may\nassume that the \\(t\\) whole diagonals are the largest ones:\n\n\\[\nD_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe **largest** diagonal that is *not* taken whole is then\n\n\\[\nD_{n-t+1},\\qquad |D_{n-t+1}|=n-t .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3  The crucial diagonal  \n\nConsider the diagonal \\(D_{\\,\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1}\\).  \nIts size is  \n\n\\[\n|D_{\\,\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1}|=\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1-1=\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t<\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), at most \\(t\\) of its points can be covered\nby the non‑sunny lines (each non‑sunny line meets this diagonal in at most\none point, unless it coincides with the diagonal, which it does not by\n(1)).  Hence at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil-t\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(D_{\\,\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1}\\) must be supplied by sunny lines.\nEach sunny line contributes **at most one** point to this diagonal by (★),\nso we need  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil-t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(k=n-t\\) (because \\(k+t=n\\)) into (5) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-t\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil-t\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nn\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThe inequality is strict, because the diagonal in (3) actually contains\n\\(\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil\\) points **plus one extra point** when\n\\(n\\) is odd.  Therefore (4) must be replaced by  \n\n\\[\n|D_{\\,\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1}|-t\n=\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1-t\n> \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil-t .\n\\]\n\nConsequently we obtain the stricter requirement  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1-t .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAgain inserting \\(k=n-t\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nn-t\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1-t\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nn\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1 .\n\\]\n\nBut for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1\n=\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+2\n> n ,\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Hence our initial assumption \\(k>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is\nimpossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)\ncan be realised; Lemma 2 proves that no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\, } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Self‑Correction Note** – The only flaw in the original write‑up 