, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\, } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=s\\},\n   \\qquad |D_s|=s-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The required set of points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Property of sunny lines.**  \n   If a line \\(L\\) is sunny, write a primitive direction vector \\((p,q)\\) with\n   \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  \n   All lattice points of \\(L\\) are \\((x_0+tp,\\;y_0+tq)\\;(t\\in\\mathbb Z)\\); their\n   sums equal \\((x_0+y_0)+t(p+q)\\).  \n   Hence two distinct points of \\(L\\) cannot have the same sum, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{A sunny line meets each diagonal }D_s\\text{ in at most one lattice point.}}\\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Upper bound \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists and put \\(t=n-k\\) (the\n   number of non‑sunny lines).  \n   Consider the diagonal \\(D_{2k+1}\\); it contains \\(2k\\) points.\n   By (★) the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) of them, so at least\n   \\(k\\) points of \\(D_{2k+1}\\) must be covered by non‑sunny lines.\n   Every non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or of the form\n   \\(x+y=c\\); each of them meets \\(D_{2k+1}\\) in **at most one** point\n   (unless it coincides with the whole diagonal, which would already give a\n   non‑sunny line covering all its points and would only *decrease* the number\n   of required non‑sunny lines).  \n   Consequently we need at least \\(k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   t=n-k\\ge k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer,  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).**  \n   Fix such a \\(k\\) and put \\(m=n-2k\\;(m\\ge0)\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x+i .\n   \\]\n\n   All \\(S_i\\) have direction \\((1,1)\\); therefore they are sunny.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the \\(m\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   X_j:\\;x+y=n+1-j\\qquad(j=0,1,\\dots ,m-1)\n   \\]\n\n   (these are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non‑sunny) and\n   the \\(k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i+1\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;m\\;(\\text{diagonals})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{vertical})\n   =k+m+k=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a-1}\\).\n   - Otherwise \\(a>k\\).  \n     If \\(a+b\\ge 2k+2\\) then the point belongs to the diagonal\n     \\(X_{\\,n+1-(a+b)}\\) (because \\(a+b\\) is one of the sums\n     \\(2k+2,2k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\) that are represented by the chosen diagonals).\n   - The remaining case is \\(a>k\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2k+1\\).  \n     Write \\(c=b-a\\).  Since \\(a>k\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2k+1\\),\n\n     \\[\n     -k+1\\le c\\le k-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Choose the index \\(i\\) with \\(i=c\\) if \\(c\\ge0\\) and with \\(i=c+k\\) if\n     \\(c<0\\); in both cases \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  Then\n     \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+i\\), i.e. it lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belong