            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n#### 1.  Notation and definition  \n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from  \n\n* \\(0\\)   (horizontal),\n* \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and\n* \\(-1\\) (the slope of the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nThus a sunny line has slope \\(m\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and is not vertical.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A necessary bound on \\(k\\)\n\nConsider the right‑angled triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R_{\\ge 0}^2\\mid x+y\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) lies inside \\(T_n\\) and has integer coordinates.\n\n*Lemma 2.1.*  \nAny straight line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line intersecting \\(T_n\\). The intersection \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) is a (possibly degenerate) line segment whose Euclidean length does not exceed the length of the longest side of \\(T_n\\), namely \\(n+1\\).  \n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points of \\(S_n\\), the distance between consecutive lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) is at least \\(1\\) (the lattice \\(\\mathbb Z^2\\) has unit spacing). Hence a segment of length at most \\(n+1\\) can contain at most \\(\\lfloor n+1\\rfloor = n+1\\) lattice points.  \n\nThe two endpoints of the segment lie on the boundary of \\(T_n\\). Neither \\((0,n+1)\\) nor \\((n+1,0)\\) belongs to \\(S_n\\) because the coordinates must be positive. Consequently at most one of the two extreme lattice points can be in \\(S_n\\), so the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) is at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\nSince  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\nany covering of \\(S_n\\) by lines needs at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{|S_n|}{n}= \\frac{n+1}{2}\n\\]\n\nlines. The problem asks for exactly \\(n\\) lines, which is certainly feasible (we shall construct such coverings).  \nBecause a sunny line is a line, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n}. \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following three‑line families are pairwise distinct, cover all points of \\(S_3\\), and contain the indicated number of sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines (equations) | Sunny lines |\n|------|-------------------|-------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=3\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\; y=1,\\; y=2x-1\\) | \\(y=2x-1\\) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\; y=2x-1,\\; y=3-2x\\) | the last two |\n| 3 | \\(y=2x-1,\\; y=3-2x,\\; y=5-2x\\) | all three |\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 3\\) is realizable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Inductive step\n\nWe prove the following statement by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n> **Induction Statement \\(\\mathcal{P}(n)\\).**  \n> For every integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe base case \\(\\mathcal{P}(3)\\) holds by the table above.\n\nAssume \\(\\mathcal{P}(m)\\) holds for some \\(m\\ge 3\\).  \nWe prove \\(\\mathcal{P}(m+1)\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le m+1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nr:=m+1-k\\qquad (0\\le r\\le m+1).\n\\]\n\n##### 4.1  Covering the first \\(r\\) columns\n\nConsider the \\(r\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,r).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are **not** sunny (their slope is \\(\\infty\\)).  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in S_{m+1}\\) with \\(a\\le r\\) we have \\(a=i\\) for some \\(i\\) in the above list, so the point lies on \\(L_i\\). Hence all points of \\(S_{m+1}\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(r\\) are covered.\n\n##### 4.2  The remaining uncovered points\n\nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{m+1}\\) is not covered yet, then \\(a>r\\). Write \\(a=r+a'\\) with \\(a'\\ge 1\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le m+2\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nr+a'+b\\le m+2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a'+b\\le (m+2)-r = k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the set of uncovered points is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n\\]\n\n##### 4.3  Applying the induction hypothesis to the sub‑triangle\n\nBy the induction hypothesis \\(\\mathcal{P}(k)\\) (note that \\(k\\le m\\) or \\(k=m+1\\); in either case the hypothesis is available), there exist \\(k\\) distinct lines that cover \\(S_k\\) and have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Consequently **all** of these \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n##### 4.4  Assembling a covering for \\(S_{m+1}\\)\n\nTake the union of the \\(r\\) vertical lines from §4.1 and the \\(k\\) sunny lines from §4.3.  \n\n* The total number of lines is \\(r+k = (m+1)\\).  \n* The vertical lines have slope \\(\\infty\\) whereas the sunny lines have finite slopes different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore the two families are disjoint, and all lines are distinct.  \n* Together they cover every point of \\(S_{m+1}\\) (the first \\(r\\) columns by the vertical lines, the rest by the sunny lines).  \n* The number of sunny lines in the whole family equals the number of sunny lines in the second part, namely \\(k\\).\n\nThus for the size \\(m+1\\) we have constructed a covering with **any** prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, \\(0\\le k\\le m+1\\). This proves \\(\\mathcal{P}(m+1)\\).\n\nBy induction, \\(\\mathcal{P}(n)\\) holds for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer\n\nFrom Lemma 2.1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\).  \nThe inductive construction shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
