ying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn particular, \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. Let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Using only the non‑sunny lines (vertical, horizontal, or of the form \\(x+y=c\\)) we can cover at most \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3\\) points of \\(S_n\\); the three points \\((n-1,1),(n,1),(n-1,2)\\) remain uncovered.  \n   Any line through two of these points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each sunny line can contain at most one of them, so at least three sunny lines are needed. Hence \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\n3. **Realising \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) cover all points except \\((n,1)\\); add any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) (e.g. \\(y=2x-(2n-1)\\)).\n\n4. **Covering a triangular lattice by exactly its size many sunny lines.**  \n\n   **Lemma 2.** For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   d_i = 2i-(m+1)\\qquad(\\text{so }d_i\\in\\{-(m-1),-(m-3),\\dots ,m-1\\}),\n   \\]\n   and consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = x-d_i\\qquad\\text{(slope }1\\text{, hence sunny)} .\n   \\]\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\in S_m\\) satisfies \\(b=a-d_i\\) exactly when \\(a-b=d_i\\).  \n   Since every point of \\(S_m\\) has a unique integer difference \\(a-b\\) belonging to the set \\(\\{-(m-1),-(m-3),\\dots ,m-1\\}\\), it lies on precisely one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \n   The lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\) are distinct (different intercepts) and together contain all points of \\(S_m\\). ∎\n\n5. **Construction for any \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(r=n-k\\).  \n   Use the \\(r\\) vertical non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r .\n   \\]\n\n   The points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by these lines are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\mid a>r,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   =\\{(a',b)\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k ,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(a'=a-r\\).  \n   By Lemma 2, \\(S_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; none of them is vertical, so they are distinct from the previously used \\(r\\) lines.  \n   Altogether we have \\(r+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and they cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n6. Combining the above, the possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nk=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{and all }k\\text{ with }3\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nFor a fixed column \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) the points of \\(S_n\\) in that column are  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(i,n+1-i\\bigr) .\n\\]\n\nIf a column is not covered by a vertical line, each of its points must be covered by a different non‑sunny line, because a horizontal line meets the column in at most one point and a line of the form \\(x+y=c\\) also meets it in at most one point.  \nHence, to maximise the number of points covered by non‑sunny lines we should take vertical lines for the columns with the largest numbers of points.  \n\nUsing the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\n\\]\n\ncovers  \n\n\\[\nn+(n-1)+\\dots +3=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-3\n\\]\n\npoints, leaving exactly the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n,1),\\qquad P_3=(n-1,2)\n\\]\n\nuncovered.  \n\nAny line through two of these points has slope \\(0\\) (through \\(P_1,P_2\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (through \\(P_1,P_3\\)) or \\(-1\\) (through \\(P_2,P_3\\)). Consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, so at least three sunny lines are necessary.  \nThus **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).** ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Realising \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe vertical lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\); the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains \\((n,1)\\). All lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThey cover every point of \\(S_n\\) except \\((n,1)\\). Add any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\ny=2x-(2n-1)\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\). Exactly one sunny line appears, so \\(k=1\\) is attainable. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering a triangular lattice by exactly its size many sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 2.** *For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set*  \n\n\\[\nS_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}\n\\]\n\n*can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nd_i = 2i-(m+1)\\in\\{-(m-1),