e  \n\n   \\[\n   j:=a-b-n+k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le j\\le k\\), and the point\n   \\((a,b)=(n-k+j+t,\\,1+t)\\) lies on \\(L_j\\).  \n   Thus every point of the right‑hand triangle is covered by one of the\n   sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   Points with \\(a\\le n-k\\) are covered by the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nHence the \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\{V_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-k}\\cup\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) satisfy the\nrequired covering condition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Optimality.*  \nObviously no configuration can contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, and the\nconstruction works for every \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  Therefore the set of\nadmissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e.\nit is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the following family of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L(k)=\\bigl\\{\\,V_i:x=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-k)\\bigr\\}\n               \\cup\n               \\bigl\\{\\,L_j:y=x-(n-k)+(j-1)\\;(j=1,\\dots ,k)\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe lines \\(V_i\\) are vertical, hence non‑sunny.  \nEach line \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny.\n\n*Coverage of the left part.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) and \\(a\\le n-k\\), then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains the\npoint, because \\(V_a\\) consists of all points with first coordinate \\(a\\).\n\n*Coverage of the right part.*  \nAssume \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  Write \\(b=1+t\\) with \\(t\\ge 0\\).  \nSince \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(