ongs to \\(T_m\\) but not to \\(T_{m-1}\\).  \n     The line through \\((1,m)\\) and \\((2,m-2)\\) has slope  \n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{m-(m-2)}{1-2}= \\frac{2}{-1}=-2,\n     \\]\n\n     which is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \n     Adding this line to \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}\\) yields a family of \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\). ∎\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *Step 1.* Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\n   \\]\n\n   (all non‑sunny). They cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2.* The remaining points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the change of variables \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\), \\(b' = b\\) this set is precisely \\(T_k\\).  \n   By the Lemma there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\); translate them back to the original coordinates.  \n\n   The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them. All lines are distinct because the vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-intercepts and each sunny line introduced in the Lemma contains the new point \\((1,m)\\) that does not belong to any previously constructed sunny line.\n\n5. **Edge cases.**  \n   – When \\(k=0\\) the construction reduces to the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n   – When \\(k=n\\) the first step uses no vertical line; the Lemma provides a covering of the whole set \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the