ace{y-1=x-1}_{\\text{sunny}},\\qquad\n  \\underbrace{y-2=-\\tfrac12(x-1)}_{\\text{sunny}},\\qquad\n  \\underbrace{y-3=-2(x-1)}_{\\text{sunny}} .\n  \\]\n\nEach family consists of three distinct lines, exactly the required number of\nthem are sunny, and every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction\n\nAssume that for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two conditions exists.\nWe show how to obtain families for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Keeping the number of sunny lines\n\nAdd the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+2}:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D_{n+2}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is **not** sunny.\nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) whose sum is \\(n+2\\) lie on \\(D_{n+2}\\); every\nother point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) already belongs to \\(S_n\\) and is covered by the\nold family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\,n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{D_{n+2}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one\n\nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n+1,k}\\) just constructed.\nThe point  \n\n\\[\nP=(1,n+1)\\in S_{n+1}\n\\]\n\nlies on the newly added diagonal \\(D_{n+2}\\) and on no other line of the\nfamily.  Replace \\(D_{n+2}\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-(n+1)=2\\,(x-1) \\qquad\\text{(slope }2\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) passes through \\(P\\) and also through the point\n\\(Q=(2,n-1)\\in S_{n+1}\\); the latter point is already covered by\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  Hence after the replacement every point of\n\\(S_{n+1}\\) is still covered, the set of lines remains of size \\(n+1\\), and\nthe num