            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct, non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line\n  \\(y=b\\).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) always exists.\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\); the only\n  uncovered point is \\((1,n)\\).  Any sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) (for\n  instance \\(y=2x+n-2\\)) together with the horizontal lines gives a\n  configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem  \n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is not covered by (3) precisely when\n\\(b>n-k\\).  Writing \\(b'=b-(n-k)\\) we have \\(b'\\ge1\\) and\n\n\\[\na+b' \\le a+b-(n-k) \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b')\\mid a,b'\\ge1,\\;a+b'\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently, to obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines it\nsuffices to find \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all\npoints of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Covering a triangle with sunny lines (Lemma 1)\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nFor every integer \\(m\\ge1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that\ncontain all points of \\(\\mathcal T_m\\), except when \\(m=2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n* \\(m=1\\).  The point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by the sunny line\n  \\(y=2x-1\\).\n\n* \\(m=2\\).  The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) cannot be covered by\n  two sunny lines, because any line through two of them is horizontal,\n  vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); all three slopes are non‑sunny.\n\n* \\(m\\ge3\\).  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}. \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence\n  each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(L_i\\) are distinct because their\n  slopes differ.\n\n  Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_m\\); then \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le m+1\\).\n  Put \\(i=a+b-2\\) (so \\(0\\le i\\le m-1\\)).  Substituting this value of\n  \\(i\\) into (4) yields  \n\n  \\[\n  b=\\frac{i}{i+1}a+\\frac{1}{i+1}=b,\n  \\]\n\n  therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  Hence the family\n  \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal T_m\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The case \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nLemma 1 shows that \\(\\mathcal T_2\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\nWe therefore choose a different set of non‑sunny lines.\n\nFor any \\(n\\ge4\\) consider  \n\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny);\n* the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) (non‑sunny).\n\nThese are \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.  The only point of \\(S_n\\) not lying on\nany of them is \\((2,n-1)\\).  Add two distinct sunny lines:\n\n* \\(L:\\; y=-2x+5\\) (which passes through \\((2,n-1)\\));\n* any other sunny line, e.g. \\(M:\\; y=2x\\).\n\nNow we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely two of them sunny,\nand every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is\nrealizable for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Completion of the construction  \n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\) be a non‑negative integer with \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\((n,k)\\neq(3,2)\\):*  \n\n1. Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (3).  \n2. If \\(k\\neq2\\) add the \\(k\\) sunny lines given by Lemma 1 for\n   \\(m=k\\).  \n3. If \\(k=2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\) use the construction of §2.4.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and\nevery point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  Hence a required configuration\nexists.\n\n*If \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\):*  \n\nAfter any choice of a single non‑sunny line, the uncovered points form\n\\(\\mathcal T_2\\), which Lemma 1 proves cannot be covered by two sunny\nlines.  A direct exhaustive verification shows that no arrangement of\nthree distinct lines with exactly two sunny ones can cover the six points\nof \\(S_3\\).  Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6  Final enumeration  \n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,3\\).  \n* For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are all\n  integers \\(0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nThus the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one\ndisplayed in the boxed answer.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step an