            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have found a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\ \\text{for all }n\\ge 4,\\qquad \nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\ \\text{for }n=3\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. the only impossible case is \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\).\n\n**b. Method sketch**  \n\n1. **Baseline covering.**  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are non‑sunny and already cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) always exists.\n\n2. **Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem.**  \n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we keep the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (these are non‑sunny).  \n   The points not covered by (1) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the integer points of a right‑angled triangle of “size’’ \\(k\\).\n\n   Consequently we have to exhibit \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\n3. **Covering a triangle with sunny lines (corrected Lemma 1).**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that contain all points of \\(\\mathcal T_m\\), **except** when \\(m=2\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n\n   *\\(m=1\\).*  The single point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by any sunny line, e.g. \\(y=2x-1\\).\n\n   *\\(m=2\\).*  The three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines, because any line through two of them is horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); all three slopes are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(m\\ge3\\).*  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le m\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y-1=\\frac{i-1}{i}\\,(x-1)\\qquad\\text{(equivalently }y=\\frac{i-1}{i}x+\\frac1i\\text{)}.\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{i}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(L_i\\) are distinct because their slopes differ.\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_m\\).  \n   If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le m\\) and the point lies on \\(L_b\\) (since \\(L_b\\) passes through \\((1,b)\\)).  \n   If \\(a\\ge2\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=b\\;(\\;1\\le b\\le m+1-a\\;).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(b\\le m+1-a\\le m\\), the index \\(i\\) is admissible.  \n   Substituting \\(i=b\\) into (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y-1=\\frac{b-1}{b}\\,(x-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   y-1=\\frac{b-1}{b}\\,(a-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;y\n   =\\frac{b-1}{b}a+\\frac1b\n   =b,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_b\\).  \n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal T_m\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\). ∎\n\n4. **The case \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that \\(\\mathcal T_2\\) cannot be covered by two sunny lines.  \n   We therefore choose a different set of non‑sunny lines:\n\n   * the vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n   * the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\).\n\n   These are \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.  The only point of \\(S_n\\) not lying on any of them is \\((2,n-1)\\).  \n   Add two distinct sunny lines, one of which passes through \\((2,n-1)\\) (e.g. \\(y=-2x+5\\)) and the other any sunny line not coinciding with the first (e.g. \\(y=2x\\)).  \n   Altogether we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely two of them sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is realizable for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n5. **Completion of the construction.**  \n\n   Let \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\) be a non‑negative integer with \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n   *If \\((n,k)\\neq(3,2)\\):*  \n\n   1. Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (1).  \n   2. If \\(k\\neq2\\) add the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma 1 for \\(m=k\\).  \n   3. If \\(k=2\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\) use the construction of step 4.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny; and every point of  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is covered.\n\n   *If \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\):*  \n\n   After any choice of a single non‑sunny line, the uncovered points form \\(\\mathcal T_2\\), which Lemma 1 proves cannot be covered by two sunny lines.  A direct exhaustive check confirms that no arrangement of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny ones can cover the six points of \\(S_3\\).  Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n6. **Final enumeration.**  \n\n   * For \\(n=3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0,1,3\\).  \n   * For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are all integers \\(0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\nThus the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one displayed in the boxed answer. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
