, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  Consequently \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=4\\) are impossible.\n\nFor \\(k=3\\) we use the four lines\n\n\\[\ny=1,\\qquad\ny=x+1,\\qquad\ny=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\qquad\ny=-2x+6 .\n\\]\n\nAll four are distinct, the last three are sunny, and together they cover all ten points.  \nThus \\(k=3\\) is attainable.  The cases \\(k=0,1\\) are handled as in \\(n=3\\).  Hence for \\(n=4\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  \\(n=5\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_5\\) has \\(15\\) points.  \nFor every \\(k=0,1,2,3,4,5\\) we give an explicit construction.\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  the five horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=5\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\):  replace \\(y=5\\) by a sunny line through \\((5,1)\\).  \n* \\(k=2\\):  use the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\) and the two sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n  \\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+7 .\n  \\]\n  These cover all fifteen points.  \n* \\(k=3\\):  use the two horizontal lines \\(y=1,2\\) and the three sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  \\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n  \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+7 .\n  \\]\n* \\(k=4\\):  use the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the four sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  \\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n  \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+7,\\qquad\n  \\ell_4:\\;y=-2x+9 .\n  \\]\n* \\(k=5\\):  the five sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\;y=x+(i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,5)\n  \\]\n  cover all points of \\(S_5\\).\n\nThus every \\(k\\le5\\) is attainable for \\(n=5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.4  \\(n=6\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_6\\) contains \\(21\\) points.  \nA sunny line can contain at most three of them:  \nthe only lines that contain four or more of the points are horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  Consequently \\(k=5,6\\) are impossible.\n\nFor the remaining values we give constructions.\n\n* \\(k=0\\):  the six horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=6\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\):  replace \\(y=6\\) by a sunny line through \\((6,1)\\).  \n* \\(k=3\\):  use the three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\) and the three sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  \\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n  \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+7 .\n  \\]\n  These cover all \\(21\\) points.  \n* \\(k=4\\):  use the two horizontal lines \\(y=1,2\\) and the four sunny lines  \n  \\[\n  \\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  \\ell_2:\\;y=-2x+5,\\qquad\n  \\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+7,\\qquad\n  \\ell_4:\\;y=-2x+9 .\n  \\]\n  They cover all points.\n\nThus for \\(n=6\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3,4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The general case \\(n\\ge 7\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny part\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines\n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny and cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The remaining triangle\n\nThe points not yet covered lie in the triangle\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\;b>n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe triangle \\(T_k\\) has side length \\(k\\) and contains exactly\n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{b=n-k+1}^{n}(n+1-b)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\nlattice points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Covering \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case \\(k=1\\).*  \n\\(T_1\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\); the line \\(y=x\\) covers it.\n\n*Induction step.*  \nAssume that \\(T_{k-1}\\) can be covered by \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  \nWhen we increase \\(k\\) to \\(k\\), the new points added to \\(T_k\\) are exactly those with \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nAll these new points lie on the sunny line\n\n\\[\nL_k:\\;y=-2x+(n+2).\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\) we have\n\\(b=-2a+(n+2)\\), so \\((a,b)\\in L_k\\).  \nThe induction hypothesis supplies \\(k-1\\) sunny lines covering the old points of \\(T_{k-1}\\); together with \\(L_k\\) we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all of \\(T_k\\).\n\nThus \\(T_k\\) is covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Completing the construction\n\nCombine the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(H_i\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).  \nAll lines are distinct (the horizontal lines are not sunny, while the sunny lines have slopes \\(1\\) or \\(-2\\) and never coincide with a horizontal line).  \nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nHence for every \\(n\\ge7\\) and every \\(k\\le n\\) the required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nCollecting the results of Sections 1 and 2 we obtain the complete list of admissible pairs \\((n,k)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{array}{ll}\nn=3:& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\\\[2mm]\nn=4:& k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\\\[2mm]\nn=5:& k\\in\\{0,1,2,3,4,5\\}\\\\[2mm]\nn=6:& k\\in\\{0,1,3,4\\}\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge 7:& k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\; .\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nThese are all the non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.  ∎"
