ve.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The value \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\nAll points of \\(B_n\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nA sunny line is defined to be non‑parallel to the three lines\n\\(y=0\\), \\(x=0\\) and \\(x+y=0\\); in particular it is **not** parallel to\n\\(x+y=n+1\\).  Hence a sunny line can intersect \\(B_n\\) in at most one\nlattice point.  Since \\(|B_n|=n\\ge 3\\), covering the whole set \\(B_n\\) would\nrequire at least \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Therefore a configuration with exactly\ntwo sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has\n  \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently it lies on the line \\(y=b\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Keep the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) and replace the line\n  \\(y=n\\) by the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell:\\;y=-(n-1)x+n .\n  \\]\n\n  The point \\((n,1)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(\\,1=-(n-1)n+n\\), so \\(\\ell\\) passes\n  through \\((n,1)\\); all other points of \\(S_n\\) have second coordinate\n  \\(\\le n-1\\) and are covered by the horizontal lines.  Thus we obtain a\n  configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reduction to a triangular set\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies 