            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all non‑negative integers not larger than \\(n\\) except \\(k=2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad   \n   B_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Every line meets the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) in at most one point; consequently each\n   line can contain at most one point of \\(B_n\\).  \n   Because \\(|B_n|=n\\), covering all points of \\(B_n\\) needs at least \\(n\\) lines.\n   If exactly two of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are\n   horizontal or vertical.  A horizontal (resp. vertical) line that meets a point\n   of \\(B_n\\) also contains **all** points of the same row (resp. column) of the\n   triangle \\(S_n\\).  Hence the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most\n   \\(2(n-2)\\) points of \\(S_n\\), while the two sunny lines together cover at most\n   \\(2\\) further points.  Since \\(|S_n|= \\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 2n-2\\) for every\n   \\(n\\ge3\\), the whole set \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered with only two sunny\n   lines.  Thus \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are all non‑sunny and clearly\n   contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) and add the sunny\n   line  \n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=-2x+(n+2).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   The line \\(\\ell\\) passes through the unique point of \\(S_n\\) with second\n   coordinate \\(n\\), namely \\((1,n)\\); all other points have second coordinate\n   \\(\\le n-1\\) and are covered by the horizontal lines.\n\n5. **Covering the triangular part for \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n   For a given \\(k\\;(3\\le k\\le n)\\) first cover the points with second\n   coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\) by the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The uncovered points form  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   a right‑angled lattice triangle of side length \\(k\\).\n\n   **Lemma 1.** *The set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly three sunny\n   lines, namely*\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\alpha &: y = x,\\\\[2pt]\n   \\beta  &: y = -2x + (k+2),\\\\[2pt]\n   \\gamma &: y = -(k-1)x + k .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   – \\(\\alpha\\) contains all points with \\(a=b\\) (they satisfy \\(a+b\\le k+1\\)).  \n   – For any integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) we have\n   \\[\n   (a,\\;k+2-2a)\\in T_k\n   \\]\n   and this point lies on \\(\\beta\\); conversely every point of \\(\\beta\\) with\n   positive coordinates belongs to \\(T_k\\).  \n   – The line \\(\\gamma\\) passes through the whole left side\n   \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k\\}\\) of the triangle; its slope\n   \\(-(k-1)\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\gamma\\) is sunny.\n   The three lines are distinct and together contain every point of \\(T_k\\). ∎\n\n   **Lemma 2.** *If a set of points can be covered by three sunny lines,\n   then it can be covered by any larger number \\(k\\ge3\\) of sunny lines.*  \n\n   *Proof.*  Starting from the three lines of Lemma 1, add \\(k-3\\) further\n   sunny lines, each passing through a point already covered (for instance\n   through \\((1,1)\\)) with a slope that has not been used before.\n   Because a line may contain points already covered, the covering property\n   is preserved while the number of sunny lines becomes exactly \\(k\\). ∎\n\n   By Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 the triangle \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\ge3\\).\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   For a given \\(n\\) and admissible \\(k\\) we use  \n\n   * the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-k}\\) (non‑sunny);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by Lemma 2 that cover \\(T_k\\).\n\n   All \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every\n   point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines.  Hence a configuration\n   exists for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only value of \\(k\\) that cannot be realised is \\(k=2\\); every other\n   integer \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.  This completes the determination\n   of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]   ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
