\\((i,m+1-i)\\) with \\(i\\ge 2\\) lies on \\(L\\).\n\nThe remaining new point is \\((1,m)\\).  Choose another rational number\n\\(\\beta\\) different from all previously used slopes (e.g. \\(\\beta=m+1\\)) and set\n\n\\[\nM:\\; y-m = \\beta(x-1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n\\(M\\) is sunny, distinct from all earlier lines, and contains \\((1,m)\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{m-1},L,M\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{m-1}\\cup D_m=S_m\\).\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n1. **Vertical (non‑sunny) part.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   They are pairwise distinct, none is sunny, and they cover every point of\n   \\(S_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By the change of variables \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k = \\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'>0,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}=S_k,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a copy of the triangular set \\(S_k\\) shifted to the right by\n   \\(n-k\\).\n\n   Apply the Lemma to \\(S_k\\); we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines\n   \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) that cover all points of \\(S_k\\).  Translating\n   these lines by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover\n   all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n3. **Verification.**  \n   The total collection  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the latter\n   \\(k\\) are sunny.  Every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to a vertical\n   column \\(x\\le n-k\\) (hence lies on some \\(V_i\\)) or to