in families for the triangle \\(S_{n+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Keeping the number of sunny lines\n\nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv:\\; x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(v\\) is non‑sunny, it is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\)\n(because every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) has \\(x\\le n\\)), and it covers the\nonly new point of \\(S_{n+1}\\), namely \\((n+1,1)\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{v\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n+1}\\) and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Increasing the number of sunny lines by one\n\nLet \\(\\alpha\\) be a rational number satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any sunny line in }\\mathcal L_{n,k}.\n\\]\n\nSuch an \\(\\alpha\\) exists because the set of forbidden slopes is finite.\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\ns:\\; y-1=\\alpha\\bigl(x-(n+1)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(s\\) passes through the new point \\((n+1,1)\\), its slope is\n\\(\\alpha\\) (hence sunny), and it is distinct from every line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(S_{n+1}\\) and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the induction\n\nFrom the base case we have configurations for \\((3,k)\\) for all\n\\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  The two constructions above show that, whenever a\nconfiguration exists for \\((n,k)\\), configurations exist for both\n\\((n+1,k)\\) and \\((n+1,k+1)\\).  By repeatedly applying the step “\\(k\\) or\n\\(k+1\\)’’ we obtain, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a family