fying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not found a complete solution. I have rigorously proven that for every integer \\(n\\ge 6\\) the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is \\(k=0\\). For \\(n=3,4,5\\) I have shown that the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n1. **Forced non‑sunny lines.**  \n   The points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) force the vertical line \\(x=1\\); the points \\((a,1)\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\) force the horizontal line \\(y=1\\). Thus every admissible family contains at least these two non‑sunny lines.\n\n2. **Reduction to a smaller triangle.**  \n   After removing the two forced lines, the remaining points form the triangle  \n   \\[\n   T_{n-2}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   We must cover \\(T_{n-2}\\) with the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines.\n\n3. **Upper bound for sunny lines.**  \n   Consider the outermost anti‑diagonal \\(S=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) of \\(T_{n-2}\\).  \n   A sunny line can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point, whereas a non‑sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) meets \\(S\\) in all its points.  \n   If a sunny line is used, then at least \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S\\) must be covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines.  \n   Since each of those lines can cover at most one point of \\(S\\), all of them must be vertical or horizontal.  \n   A mixture of vertical and horizontal lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_{n-2}\\) simultaneously, because points with both coordinates \\(\\ge 2\\) would remain uncovered.  \n   Hence **no sunny line can appear** when \\(n\\ge 6\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(n\\ge 6\\).**  \n   For every \\(n\\ge 6\\) the family  \n   \\[\n   \\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4,\\;x+y=5,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\,\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers all points of the required set.  \n   Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable, and by the previous step it is the only possible value.\n\n5. **Small cases \\(n=3,4,5\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3,4,5\\) the outermost anti‑diagonal has only \\(2,3,4\\) points respectively, so the argument in step 3 does not force the absence of sunny lines.  \n   Explicit constructions show that \\(k=1\\) is attainable in each of these cases, while \\(k=0\\) is always attainable by the construction of step 4.  \n   No configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) can exist because the remaining triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\) would then require more lines than available to cover all its points.\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nWe prove the two statements separately.\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Forced non‑sunny lines\n\nThe points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\); hence any family of lines covering all required points must contain \\(x=1\\).  \nSimilarly, the points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) lie on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\); thus \\(y=1\\) must also belong to the family.  \nThese two lines are distinct and non‑sunny (their slopes are \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\)\n\nAfter removing the two forced lines, the remaining points are\n\\[\nT_{n-2}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nThe number of points in \\(T_{n-2}\\) is\n\\[\n|T_{n-2}|=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\nWe still have \\(n-2\\) lines to use for covering \\(T_{n-2}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  No sunny line for \\(n\\ge 6\\)\n\nLet \\(S=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) be the outermost anti‑diagonal of \\(T_{n-2}\\).  \nIt contains exactly \\(n-1\\) points.\n\n*Lemma 3.1.*  \nA sunny line can meet \\(S\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a sunny line met \\(S\\) in two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\), then\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=\\bigl((n+1)-a_2\\bigr)-\\bigl((n+1)-a_1\\bigr)=-(a_2-a_1),\n\\]\nso the slope of the line would be \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\nAssume that a sunny line \\(L\\) is used.  \nSince \\(L\\) meets \\(S\\) in at most one point, the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the other \\(n-2\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nEach of those lines can cover at most one point of \\(S\\) (by Lemma 3.1), so all of them must be vertical or horizontal.  \nConsequently, the set of these \\(n-1\\) lines consists of some vertical lines \\(x=c\\) and some horizontal lines \\(y=d\\).\n\nLet \\(V\\) be the set of \\(c\\)-values of the vertical lines and \\(H\\) the set of \\(d\\)-values of the horizontal lines.  \nBecause the lines are distinct, \\(|V|+|H|=n-1\\).  \nEvery point of \\(S\\) has either its \\(x\\)-coordinate in \\(V\\) or its \\(y\\)-coordinate in \\(H\\); hence\n\\[\nV\\cup H=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(|V|+|H|=n-1\\) forces \\(|V|=n-1\\) or \\(|H|=n-1\\).  \nSuppose \\(|V|=n-1\\); then all vertical lines are \\(x=2,3,\\dots ,n\\).  \nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_{n-2}\\) whose first coordinate is at least \\(2\\).  \nThe only points that remain uncovered are those with first coordinate \\(1\\), i.e. \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \nBut none of the vertical lines cover such points, and we have no horizontal lines left (since \\(|H|=0\\)).  \nHence these points would remain uncovered – a contradiction.  \nThe case \\(|H|=n-1\\) is symmetric.\n\nTherefore **no sunny line can appear** in any admissible family when \\(n\\ge 6\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for \\(n\\ge 6\\)\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 6\\) the following \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines cover all required points:\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4,\\;x+y=5,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThe first two lines are the forced ones.  \nEach line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(4\\le c\\le n+1\\) is non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies either on \\(x=1\\), on \\(y=1\\), or on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  \nThus all points are covered, and the family contains no sunny lines.  \nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable, and by §3 it is the only possible value.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Small values \\(n=3,4,5\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3,4,5\\) the outermost anti‑diagonal \\(S\\) has only \\(2,3,4\\) points respectively, so the argument of §3 does not forbid sunny lines.  \nExplicit constructions show that \\(k=1\\) is attainable in each case:\n\n| \\(n\\) | Construction (lines) | Sunny lines |\n|-------|----------------------|-------------|\n| 3 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x-y=0\\) | 1 |\n| 4 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=5,\\;x-y=0\\) | 1 |\n| 5 | \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=6,\\;x+y=5,\\;x-y=0\\) | 1 |\n\nIn all three cases the family contains exactly one sunny line and covers all points.  \nThe construction of §4 shows that \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \nNo configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) can exist because the remaining triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\) would then require more lines than available to cover all its points.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}, & n=3,4,5,\\\\[2mm]\nk=0, & n\\ge 6.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
