ts on a sunny line.**  \n   Let a sunny line meet the axes at \\((p,0)\\) and \\((0,q)\\) with positive integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(p\\neq q\\).  \n   Writing \\(p=g p',\\;q=g q'\\) with \\(\\gcd(p',q')=1\\), the lattice points on the line are  \n\n   \\[\n   (p-gp't,\\;q-gq't),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,g .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the line contains exactly \\(g-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the two axis‑intercepts are not in \\(S_n\\)).  \n   Since \\(p,q\\le n\\), we have \\(g\\le\\min\\{p,q\\}\\le n\\); therefore a sunny line contains **at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n\n5. **Excluding sunny lines for \\(n\\ge 6\\).**  \n   From \\(|V|+|H|=n-s\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   |A|+|B|=2n-(n-s)=n+s+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   By the arithmetic–geometric mean inequality,\n\n   \\[\n   |A|\\;|B|\\ge\\Bigl(\\frac{|A|+|B|}{2}\\Bigr)^{\\!2}\n            =\\Bigl(\\frac{n+s+1}{2}\\Bigr)^{\\!2}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Every pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\in A,\\;b\\in B\\) belongs to the right‑hand side of (1); thus the sunny lines together must contain at least \\(|A|\\;|B|\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Using the bound from step 4 we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl(\\frac{n+s+1}{2}\\Bigr)^{\\!2}\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 6\\) the left‑hand side already exceeds \\(n-1\\) when \\(s\\ge 1\\) (e.g. for \\(n=6,s=1\\) we have \\((7/2)^2=12.25>5\\)).  \n   Hence (4) cannot hold; therefore **\\(s=0\\)**, i.e. no sunny line can appear when \\(n\\ge 6\\).\n\n6. **At most one sunny line for \\(n=3,4,5\\).**  \n   The same inequality (4) with \\(n=3,4,5\\) shows that it fails already for \\(s\\ge 2\\); consequently a covering can contain **at most one** sunny line for these three values of \\(n\\).\n\n7. **Existence of the admissible values.**  \n\n   *All non‑sunny lines.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L^{(0)}_n=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines (all non‑sunny) and clearly covers every point of \\(S_