            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\(\\#(L)\\) the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Basic bounds\n\n*If \\(L\\) is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) (a non‑sunny line) then  \n\\(\\#(L)\\le n\\).*\n\nIndeed, a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\), hence at most \\(n\\) of them; the same holds for horizontal lines.  \nA diagonal line \\(x+y=d\\) contains the points \\((a,d-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\); since \\(d\\le n+1\\), again \\(\\#(L)\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n*If \\(L\\) is sunny then \\(\\#(L)\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).*\n\nLet the slope of \\(L\\) be \\(p/q\\) in lowest terms (\\(q>0\\)).  \nWrite an integer point on \\(L\\) as \\((x_0+ kq,\\;y_0+kp)\\) with \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nThe condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(x_0+y_0)+k(p+q)\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x_0+y_0\\ge 2\\), the largest admissible \\(k\\) is at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{p+q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nHence the number of integer points on \\(L\\) inside the triangle is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{p+q}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(p+q\\ge 2\\).  The bound is attained by the line \\(y=x\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of coverings with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) are all non‑sunny and each of them contains exactly the points of \\(S_n\\) with the corresponding abscissa.  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of these lines.\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  For any \\(n\\ge 3\\) consider the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=2x-(2n-1).\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(L\\) has slope \\(2\\) and passes through the point \\((n,1)\\); it contains no other point of \\(S_n\\).  \n  Every point of \\(S_n\\) with abscissa \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); the only point with abscissa \\(n\\) is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(L\\).  Thus all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of two or more sunny lines\n\nAssume that a covering of \\(S_n\\) with \\(n\\) distinct lines contains at least two sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\ns\\ge 2\\quad\\text{(number of sunny lines)},\\qquad\nt=n-s\\quad\\text{(number of non‑sunny lines)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The anti‑diagonal\n\nThe points of the anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nA_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nare \\(n\\) in number and are pairwise non‑collinear with slope \\(-1\\).  \nA sunny line meets this anti‑diagonal in at most one point, because two distinct points of the anti‑diagonal determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \nHence at most \\(s\\) points of \\(A_n\\) are covered by sunny lines, and the remaining \\(n-s\\) points must be covered by non‑sunny lines.\n\nA non‑sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(A_n\\) (a vertical line \\(x=c\\) covers the point \\((c,n+1-c)\\); a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) covers the point \\((n+1-c,c)\\); a diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) covers all of \\(A_n\\) but then no other anti‑diagonal point is left).  \nBecause we have exactly \\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny lines, the only possibility is that **every** non‑sunny line covers exactly one point of \\(A_n\\).  \nConsequently all non‑sunny lines are either vertical or horizontal, and each of them is of the form \\(x=c\\) or \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Points with \\(a+b\\le n\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe \\(t=n-s\\) non‑sunny lines can cover at most \\(t\\,n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (each of them contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\)).  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered by the \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \nBy the bound of §1.2 each sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most\n\n\\[\nt\\,n+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=(n-s)n+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  A contradiction\n\nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(s\\ge 2\\) one checks directly that\n\n\\[\n(n-s)n+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, write \\(n=2m\\) or \\(n=2m+1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) (even).*  \nThen \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and (1) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2m-s)2m+s\\,m=2m^2-m\\,s .\n\\]\n\nInequality (2) is  \n\n\\[\n2m^2-m\\,s<\\frac{2m(2m-1)}{2}=m(2m-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(2m^2-m\\,s<m(2m-1)\\), which simplifies to \\(s>1\\).  This holds because \\(s\\ge 2\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).*  \nThen \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and (1) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1-s)(2m+1)+s(m+1)\n= (2m+1)^2 - s\\,m .\n\\]\n\nInequality (2) is  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)^2 - s\\,m < \\frac{(2m+1)(2m)}{2}= (2m+1)m ,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((2m+1)^2 - s\\,m < (2m+1)m\\), which reduces to \\(s>1\\).  Again this holds because \\(s\\ge 2\\).\n\nThus (2) is true for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(s\\ge 2\\).  \nBut (2) says that the points of \\(T_n\\) cannot all be covered, contradicting the assumption that the \\(n\\) lines form a covering of \\(S_n\\).\n\nTherefore a covering of \\(S_n\\) with \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain **at most one** sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining the constructions of §2 with the impossibility of \\(s\\ge 2\\) proved in §3, we conclude that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\,}\n\\]\n\nare exactly the non‑negative integers for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
