$y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation and elementary bounds** –  \n   *\\(S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is the set of lattice points that must be covered; \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).  \n   *A *non‑sunny* line is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); a *sunny* line has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   *If a line is non‑sunny then it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *If a line is sunny and its direction vector is \\((q,p)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) then the sum \\(a+b\\) of the lattice points on the line increases by \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) at each step; consequently a sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0,1\\)** –  \n   *\\(k=0\\): the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\): the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=2x-(2n-1)\\) (slope \\(2\\)) cover all points; the latter line contains only the point \\((n,1)\\) of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)** –  \n   Let \\(s\\ge 2\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.  \n   Split the set \\(S_n\\) into  \n\n   *the *anti‑diagonal* \\(A_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) (size \\(n\\)), and  \n   *the remaining points \\(T_n=S_n\\setminus A_n\\) (size \\(|T_n|=n(n-1)/2\\)).  \n\n   **(i) Covering the anti‑diagonal.**  \n   A line that is not the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) meets \\(A_n\\) in at most one point, because any two points of \\(A_n\\) determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence either  \n\n   *the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is used (it is non‑sunny), or  \n   *each point of \\(A_n\\) is covered by a distinct line.\n\n   **(ii) The diagonal is *not* used.**  \n   Then the \\(n\\) points of \\(A_n\\) are covered by \\(n\\) distinct lines, one per point.  \n   Every such line already contains one point of \\(A_n\\); the remaining points it can contain belong to \\(T_n\\).  \n   For a sunny line the bound of §1 gives at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\); for a non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) but not the diagonal) the bound is at most \\(n-1\\).  \n   Consequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (t)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|T_n|=n(n-1)/2\\), inequality (1) would have to satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   (t)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(t=n-s\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n(n-1)-s(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n   \\]\n   i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\\Bigr)\\ge-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\\le -\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).  \n   Hence the left‑hand side of (3) is at most \\(-s\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).  \n   Because \\(s\\ge 2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   -s\\frac{n-1}{2}\\le -\\frac{n-1}{2}<-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   which contradicts (3).  Therefore the diagonal cannot be omitted.\n\n   **(iii) The diagonal *is* used.**  \n   Suppose the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) belongs to the family; it is non‑sunny and already covers all \\(n\\) points of \\(A_n\\).  \n   We are left with \\(t-1=n-s-1\\) non‑sunny lines (all vertical or horizontal) and the \\(s\\) sunny lines to cover the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n'=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n'|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   A vertical (or horizontal) line \\(x=c\\) (resp. \\(y=c\\)) now contains at most \\(n-c\\) (resp. \\(c-1\\)) points of \\(T_n'\\); in any case it contains at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \n   Hence the total number of points of \\(T_n'\\) that can be covered is bounded by  \n\n   \\[\n   (t-1)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(t=n-s\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-s-1)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The left‑hand side of (5) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)^2-s(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)^2-s(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\le (n-1)^2-s\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(s\\ge 2\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right‑hand side is strictly smaller than \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\); a direct computation (separating the cases \\(n=2m\\) and \\(n=2m+1\\)) shows the inequality fails.  \n   Hence (5) cannot hold, and a covering with \\(s\\ge 2\\) sunny lines is impossible.\n\n   The two sub‑cases together prove that **no configuration with \\(s\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists**.\n\n4. **Conclusion** –  \n   The constructions of §2 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable, while the argument of §3 shows that \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Elementary bounds\n\n*If \\(L\\) is non‑sunny then \\(\\#(L\\cap S_n)\\le n\\).*  \nIndeed a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); the number of such points is at most \\(n\\).  The same bound holds for a horizontal line and for a line of slope \\(-1\\) (the diagonal \\(x+y=d\\) contains the points \\((a,d-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\le n\\)).\n\n*If \\(L\\) is sunny then \\(\\#(L\\cap S_n)\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).*  \nWrite the direction vector of \\(L\\) as \\((q,p)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nAll integer points of \\(L\\) have the form \\((x_0+kq,\\;y_0+kp)\\) \\((k\\in\\mathbb Z)\\).  \nFor such a point the sum \\(x+y\\) equals \\((x_0+y_0)+k(p+q)\\).  \nSince \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\) on \\(S_n\\), the integer \\(k\\) can take at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(x_0+y_0)}{p+q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{p+q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(p+q\\ge 2\\).  Hence the claimed bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny.  For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) the abscissa \\(a\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), therefore the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=2x-(2n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L\\) has slope \\(2\\) (hence it is sunny) and passes through the single point \\((n,1)\\) of \\(S_n\\); no other point of \\(S_n\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L\\).  All remaining points have abscissa at most \\(n-1\\) and are covered by the corresponding vertical line.  Hence the whole set \\(S_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  No covering with two or more sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and that  \n\n\\[\ns:=|\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\\ge 2 .\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\\qquad |A_n|=n,\n\\]\n\\[\nT_n=S_n\\setminus A_n,\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Covering the anti‑diagonal \\(A_n\\)\n\nAny line that is **not** the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) meets \\(A_n\\) in at most one point, because two distinct points of \\(A_n\\) determine a line of slope \\(-1\\).  Consequently we have two mutually exclusive possibilities.\n\n*Case 1 – the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is **not** used.*  \nThen each point of \\(A_n\\) is covered by a distinct line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Hence the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are in one‑to‑one correspondence with the points of \\(A_n\\).\n\n*Case 2 – the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) **is** used.*  \nThe diagonal is a non‑sunny line and already covers all points of \\(A_n\\).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b\\le n\\},\\qquad |T_n'|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Impossibility of Case 1\n\nIn Case 1 each line already contains one point of \\(A_n\\).  Apart from that point a line can contain at most  \n\n* \\(\\displaystyle n-1\\) further points if it is non‑sunny (vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) but not the diagonal),  \n* \\(\\displaystyle \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) further points if it is sunny (the bound of §1 applied to the triangle \\(a+b\\le n\\)).\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(t)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\\text{where }t=n-s .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|T_n|=n(n-1)/2\\), inequality (3.1) would have to satisfy  \n\n\\[\n(n-s)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nRewrite (3.2) as  \n\n\\[\ns\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\\Bigr)\\ge-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\\le-\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).  \nThus the left‑hand side of (3.3) is at most \\(-s\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).  \nSince \\(s\\ge 2\\),\n\n\\[\n-s\\frac{n-1}{2}\\le -\\frac{n-1}{2}<-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (3.3).  Therefore Case 1 cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Impossibility of Case 2\n\nAssume now that the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nRemove this line; we are left with  \n\n\\[\nt-1=n-s-1\\quad\\text{non‑sunny lines (all vertical or horizontal)},\n\\]\n\\[\ns\\quad\\text{sunny lines},\n\\]\n\nwhich must cover the set \\(T_n'\\) of size \\(n(n-1)/2\\).\n\nA vertical (or horizontal) line \\(x=c\\) (resp. \\(y=c\\)) meets \\(T_n'\\) in at most \\(n-1\\) points, because the condition \\(a+b\\le n\\) removes the point \\((c,n+1-c)\\) that belonged to the anti‑diagonal.  Consequently the total number of points of \\(T_n'\\) that can be covered is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n(t-1)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(t=n-s\\) we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n\\[\n(n-s-1)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side of (3.5) equals  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)^2-s(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n(n-1)^2-s(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\le (n-1)^2-s\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nNow treat the two parity cases.\n\n*If \\(n=2m\\) (even):* the right‑hand side becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2m-1)^2-s(m-1)=4m^{2}-4m+1-s(m-1).\n\\]\n\nInequality (3.5) would require this to be at least \\(m(2m-1)=2m^{2}-m\\).  \nSubtracting the right‑hand side from the left gives  \n\n\\[\n2m^{2}-3m+1-s(m-1)\\ge 0.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(m\\ge 2\\) (i.e. \\(n\\ge4\\)) and \\(s\\ge2\\) the expression is negative, a contradiction.\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd):* the bound becomes  \n\n\\[\n(2m)^{2}-s m =4m^{2}-s m .\n\\]\n\nInequality (3.5) would require  \n\n\\[\n4m^{2}-s m\\ge m(2m+1)=2m^{2}+m,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(2m^{2}-(s+1)m\\ge0\\).  For \\(m\\ge1\\) and \\(s\\ge2\\) the left‑hand side is negative, again a contradiction.\n\nThus Case 2 is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4  Conclusion of the impossibility proof\n\nBoth possibilities for covering the anti‑diagonal lead to contradictions when \\(s\\ge2\\).  Hence a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain **at most one sunny line**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nThe constructions of §2 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nThe argument of §3 proves that no covering with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in 