            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bounds for a single line\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\).  \nIf \\(d\\le n\\) it meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((a,d-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\); thus it contains at most \\(d-1\\le n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nIf \\(d=n+1\\) it is the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nHence every non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet a sunny line have direction vector \\((q,p)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\\;p\\neq0\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  \nAll integer points on the line are \\((x_0+kq,\\;y_0+kp)\\) for \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nFor such a point the sum \\(x+y\\) equals \\((x_0+y_0)+k(p+q)\\).  \nSince \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\) on \\(S_n\\), the integer \\(k\\) can take at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\]\n\nvalues.  \nBecause \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) for a sunny line, this number is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nThus every sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The anti‑diagonal\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA_n=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\qquad |A_n|=n .\n\\]\n\nAny two distinct points of \\(A_n\\) determine the line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nConsequently a line that is **not** the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) meets \\(A_n\\) in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Two cases\n\nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines in a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\), and \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 1 – the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is **not** used\n\nThen each of the \\(n\\) lines contains exactly one point of \\(A_n\\).  \nAll remaining points lie in  \n\n\\[\nT_n=S_n\\setminus A_n,\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line that is not the diagonal can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (it misses the unique point of \\(A_n\\) that it already covers).  \nA sunny line can cover at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nt\\,(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe must have \\((1)\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).  \nSubstituting \\(t=n-s\\) gives\n\n\\[\n(n-s)(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\ns\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\\Bigr)\n\\;\\ge\\;-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\n\\;\\le\\;-\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the left–hand side of (3) is at most \\(-s\\,\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).  \nFor \\(s\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\n-s\\,\\frac{n-1}{2}\\;\\le\\;-\\frac{n-1}{2}\n\\;<\\;-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (3).  \nHence **Case 1 is impossible when \\(s\\ge2\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 2 – the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) **is** used\n\nThe diagonal is a non‑sunny line and covers all of \\(A_n\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the set \\(T_n=S_n\\setminus A_n\\).  \nA vertical or horizontal line can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nA sunny line can cover at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(t-1)\\,(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t=n-s\\).  \nWe must have \\((4)\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).  \nSubstituting \\(t=n-s\\) gives\n\n\\[\n(n-s-1)(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\ns\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\\Bigr)\n\\;\\ge\\;-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\n\\;\\le\\;-\\frac{n-2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the left–hand side of (6) is at most \\(-s\\,\\frac{n-2}{2}\\).  \nFor \\(s\\ge2\\),\n\n\\[\n-s\\,\\frac{n-2}{2}\\;\\le\\;-\\frac{n-2}{2}\n\\;<\\;-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (6).  \nHence **Case 2 is also impossible when \\(s\\ge2\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nBoth possibilities for covering the anti‑diagonal lead to a 