                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** when its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  How many points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on a single line?\n\nWrite a line in the form  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; (x,y)=(x_0,y_0)+k(q,p),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), \\(q>0\\), and the direction vector \\((q,p)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p+q\\neq0 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe three conditions are exactly the negation of “parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)’’; hence (1) characterises\nsunny lines.\n\nFor a point of \\(L\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nx+y=(x_0+y_0)+k(p+q).                                   \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause every point of \\(S_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\), the integer\nparameter \\(k\\) can take at most  \n\n\\[\nN_1:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1                \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ndifferent values.\n\nIn addition, the \\(x\\)-coordinate must stay in the interval\n\\(1\\le x\\le n\\); using \\(x=x_0+kq\\) we obtain the bound  \n\n\\[\nN_2:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .                \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly the \\(y\\)-coordinate gives  \n\n\\[\nN_3:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .                \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\nN:=\\min\\{N_1,N_2,N_3\\}                                         \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma  \nFor every sunny line \\(L\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nN\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause of (1) we have two possibilities.\n\n*Case A.* \\(|p|=|q|=1\\).  \nThen \\(|p+q|=2\