-----------------\n#### Case 1 – the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is **not** used  \n\nEach of the \\(n\\) lines contains exactly one point of \\(A_n\\); the\nremaining points form  \n\n\\[\nT_n=S_n\\setminus A_n,\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line that is not the diagonal can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points\nof \\(T_n\\) (it already uses one point of \\(A_n\\)).  \nBy the Lemma, a sunny line can cover at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nt\\,(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .          \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWe must have (8) \\(\\ge |T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\).  Substituting \\(t=n-s\\)\ngives  \n\n\\[\n(n-s)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .                                         \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\ns\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\\Bigr)\n\\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .                                       \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\\le\n-\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the left‑hand side of (10) is at most \\(-s\\frac{n-1}{2}\\).\nIf \\(s\\ge2\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n-s\\frac{n-1}{2}\\le -\\frac{n-1}{2}\n   < -\\frac{n(n-1)}2,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (10).  Consequently **\\(s\\le1\\)** in this case.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 2 – the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) **is** used  \n\nThe diagonal is a non‑sunny line and already covers all points of\n\\(A_n\\).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover \\(T_n\\).\n\nFor a line different from the diagonal, at most \\(n-1\\) points of\n\\(T_n\\) can lie on it (the same reasoning as above).  By the Lemma,\na sunny line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(T_n\\) (the sum \\(x+y\\) now