            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (how many points of \\(S_n\\) a single line can contain)\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   *Vertical* line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with  \n   \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   The same bound holds for a *horizontal* line.  \n   A line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\).  \n   If \\(d=n+1\\) it contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n otherwise\n   \\(d\\le n\\) and it contains at most \\(d-1\\le n-1\\) points.  \n   Consequently **every non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Write a sunny line in the parametric form  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;(x,y)=(x_0+kq,\\;y_0+kp),\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), \\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\) (the last\n   condition excludes slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   For a point of \\(L\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=(x_0+y_0)+k(p+q).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\) on \\(S_n\\), the integer \\(k\\) can take at most  \n\n   \\[\n   N_1:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   values.\n\n   *If \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\)*, then \\(N_1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *If \\(|p+q|=1\\)*, then \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) have opposite signs and\n   \\(|p|\\) and \\(|q|\\) differ by exactly one.  Write  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\min\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ (\\ge1),\\qquad M:=m+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The direction vector of \\(L\\) is either \\((-M,m)\\) or \\((M,-m)\\).\n   Starting from any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\),\n\n   * moving in the direction that **decreases** the larger coordinate can be\n     performed at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{a-1}{M}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) steps before\n     the \\(x\\)-coordinate becomes non‑positive;\n   * moving in the opposite direction can be performed at most\n     \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{b-1}{M}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) steps before the\n     \\(y\\)-coordinate becomes non‑positive.\n\n   Hence the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(L\\) does not exceed  \n\n   \\[\n   N_2:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{a-1}{M}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n        \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{b-1}{M}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(M\\ge2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   N_2\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{a+b-2}{M}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n        \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n        =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining the two sub‑cases, **every sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).** ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (how many points of \\(T_n\\) a sunny line can contain)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\\qquad\nT_n:=S_n\\setminus A_n .\n\\]\n\nPoints of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le n\\).  \nRepeating the argument that led to (2)–(4) with the sum range shortened\nfrom \\([2,n+1]\\) to \\([2,n]\\) replaces the numerator \\(n-1\\) in (2) by\n\\(n-2\\).  Consequently a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints.  Hence **a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).** ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting the points\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines,\n* \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nThe set \\(A_n\\) (the “anti‑diagonal’’) consists of the \\(n\\) points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Any line that is **not** the diagonal\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) meets \\(A_n\\) in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 1 – the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is **not** used\n\nThen each of the \\(n\\) lines meets \\(A_n\\) in exactly one point; the\nremaining points form  \n\n\\[\nT_n=S_n\\setminus A_n,\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n* A non‑sunny line that is not the diagonal can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points\n  of \\(T_n\\) (it already uses one point of \\(A_n\\)).\n* By Lemma 2 a sunny line can cover at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nt\\,(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\nt\\,(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(t=n-s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-s)(n-1)+s\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nRe‑arranging,\n\n\\[\ns\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(n-1)\\Bigr)\n\\ge-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}