\).\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n(t-1)\\,(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\), we need  \n\n\\[\n(t-1)(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nWith \\(t=n-s\\) this becomes exactly the same inequality (8) as in Case 1,\nand the same reasoning leads again to the conclusion **\\(s\\le1\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nIn every admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\) there are **at most one sunny line**.  Therefore the only\npossible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions showing attainability\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots,\\;x=n .\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so it lies on the\n  line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non‑sunny.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the\n  sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  y=2x-(2n-1),\n  \\]\n\n  which passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) of \\(S_n\\) not covered by\n  the vertical lines.  Its slope is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence it is sunny.\n  Exactly one sunny line appears.\n\nBoth constructions satisfy the covering requirement, so the values\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\text{ or }1\\,}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
