-----------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (how many points of \\(T_n\\) a sunny line can contain)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\\qquad\nT_n:=S_n\\setminus A_n .\n\\]\n\nPoints of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le x+y\\le n\\).  \nRepeating the argument that led to (2)–(4) with the sum range shortened\nfrom \\([2,n+1]\\) to \\([2,n]\\) replaces the numerator \\(n-1\\) in (2) by\n\\(n-2\\).  Consequently a sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints.  Hence **a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).** ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting the points\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines,\n* \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nThe set \\(A_n\\) (the “anti‑diagonal’’) consists of the \\(n\\) points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Any line that is **not** the diagonal\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) meets \\(A_n\\) in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case 1 – the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) is **not** used\n\nThen each of the \\(n\\) lines meets \\(A_n\\) in exactly one point; the\nremaining points form  \n\n\\[\nT_n=S_n\\setminus A_n,\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n* A non‑sunny line that is not the diagonal can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points\n  of \\(T_n\\) (it already uses one point of \\(A_n\\)).\n* By Lemma 2 a sunny line can cover at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nt\\,(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\nt\\,(n-1)+s\\,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSubstituti