a partition of \\(S_n\\); their cardinalities are  \n\n\\[\n|D_c|=\\min\\{c-1,\\;n+1-c\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn particular  \n\n\\[\n|D_{n+1}|=n,\\qquad\n|D_c|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\quad\\text{for }c\\le n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is **not** parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it meets\neach anti‑diagonal \\(D_c\\) in **at most one** point.  Consequently a sunny\nline can contain at most one point of each \\(D_c\\) and, by Lemma 1, at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\) altogether.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Bounding the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\) and let  \n\n\\[\ns:=\\text{number of sunny lines},\\qquad\nt:=n-s\\;(\\text{non‑sunny lines}).\n\\]\n\nBecause the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals are disjoint, each line can meet a given\nanti‑diagonal in at most one point.  Hence **every anti‑diagonal must be\ncovered by exactly one of the \\(n\\) lines**.\n\n*The anti‑diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\).*  \nIts size is \\(n\\).  By Lemma 1 a non‑sunny line can contain all its points,\nwhereas a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n< n\\) points of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  Therefore **\\(D_{n+1}\\) must be covered by a\nnon‑sunny line**; in particular at least one non‑sunny line is present.\n\n*The remaining anti‑diagonals.*  \nFor every \\(c\\le n\\) we have \\(|D_c|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\nby (5).  A sunny line can contribute at most one point to each such\n\\(D_c\\); a non‑sunny line can also contribute at most one point because\ndifferent lines are distinct.  Consequently, to cover all points of the\n\\(n-1\\) anti‑diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_n\\) we need at least \\(n-1\\) distinct\nlines.\n\nSince one of the \\(n\\) lines is already forced to be non‑sunny (the line\ncovering \\(D_{n+1}\\)), the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are exactly the lines\nthat cover the anti‑diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_n\\).  If we had **two or more**\nsunny lines, then at least one of the anti‑diagonals \\(D_c\\;(c\\le n)\\) would\nreceive a point from a sunny line and a point from a non‑sunny line, which\nwould leave fewer than \\(|D_c|\\) points available for the other lines.\nBecause \\(|D_c|\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and a sunny line\ncontributes at most one point to each \\(D_c\\), the total number of points\nthat can be supplied to the \\(n-1\\) anti‑diagonals by **two** sunny lines\nis at most  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot (n-1) \\;<\\; \\sum_{c=2}^{n}|D_c|\n   =|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 ,\n\\]\n\nthe last inequality being strict for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence the\nanti‑diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_n\\) cannot be completely covered if\n\\(s\\ge2\\).\n\nTherefore **\\(s\\le1\\)**; i.e. in any admissible configuration there is at\nmost one sunny line.  The two possibilities are\n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Constructions attaining the two values\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots,\\;x=n .\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so it lies on the\n  line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non‑sunny.\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the\n  sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  y=2x-(2n-1),\n  \\]\n\n  which passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) of \\(S_n\\) not covered by\n  the vertical lines.  Its slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\n  hence the line is sunny.  Exactly one sunny line appears.\n\nBoth constructions satisfy the covering requirement, so the values\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\text{ or }1\\,}\n\\]"
